# The rule § FFI states about parameters, made to fire (CLAUDE.md §9: every
# check has a test that makes it fire; panel 051).
#
# `abs` takes a C int, so the binding declares `i32` — the header's own width.
# The point of writing it that way is what happens on the next line: an `i64`
# argument is refused *here*, in the frontend, by §4.3's rule against implicit
# conversion. Bound as `i64` instead, the same program compiles, runs, and
# prints 5 for 4294967301, because clang narrows the argument in silence and the
# `_Static_assert` that checks an `extern` only ever asked about the result.
#
# That asymmetry is the sentence panel 051 repaired, and it is principled: a
# result may be wider than C's, because every C integer narrower than 64 bits
# fits an `i64`; a parameter may not be, because the conversion runs the other
# way.

extern "stdlib.h"
    function abs(n: i32) -> i32

function main()
    wide: i64 @ 7
    print(abs(wide))   #~ type_mismatch
